Thank you both for your replies.

Yes, my second question was about concern that the structure of the hierarchical models is such that the abundance portion of the model is influencing plot level estimates from ranef (), rather than just returning detection corrected abundance estimates. ie the ~1 for lambda is not just an empty model, but is also influenceing the ranef() estimates.

Because I am modelling all species together to answer the question "Is passerine bird density affected by noisey and non-noisy disturbances differently", I combined detections into 3 species groups according to similar EDR and singing rates and was estimating a unique detection function for each group. I intended to then combine the corrected density estimates into a single data set to run models with density as the response and disturbance covariates. I understand this is a clunky approach, but also understand that observation level covariates cannot be fit with gdistsamp. I would want to fit species or species group as a covariate associated with each detection to deal with this in one hierarchical model. (I'm not sure I mentioned in my first post that plots were sampled twice- hence the use of gdistsamp).

Is there a way to fit a species group covariate to individual detections? I would much prefer to do the full analysis in a hierarchical model framework.

cheers
Kim

